1
00:00:00,720 --> 00:00:02,290
What did Ilya see?

2
00:00:02,770 --> 00:00:04,050
Ilya Sutskever helped

3
00:00:04,090 --> 00:00:05,040
create the AI

4
00:00:05,080 --> 00:00:06,990
that answers your questions today.

5
00:00:07,290 --> 00:00:09,440
Then he walked away from OpenAI,

6
00:00:09,480 --> 00:00:11,480
the company behind ChatGPT.

7
00:00:11,780 --> 00:00:13,040
It began on a Friday,

8
00:00:13,080 --> 00:00:14,220
a few years ago:

9
00:00:14,350 --> 00:00:16,200
a video call with his own boss,

10
00:00:16,240 --> 00:00:17,710
the head of OpenAI.

11
00:00:18,050 --> 00:00:19,950
And it was the boss who was fired.

12
00:00:20,170 --> 00:00:21,130
Soon, the whole

13
00:00:21,170 --> 00:00:22,450
internet was asking:

14
00:00:22,490 --> 00:00:24,010
what did Ilya see?

15
00:00:24,240 --> 00:00:26,650
The guess: a machine so powerful

16
00:00:26,690 --> 00:00:27,410
that it scared

17
00:00:27,450 --> 00:00:28,660
the man who built it.

18
00:00:28,830 --> 00:00:30,030
And if it scared him,

19
00:00:30,070 --> 00:00:31,340
it concerns you.

20
00:00:31,600 --> 00:00:32,900
Now a Hollywood movie

21
00:00:32,940 --> 00:00:34,450
retells that story.

22
00:00:35,000 --> 00:00:36,240
But the answer wasn't

23
00:00:36,280 --> 00:00:37,570
written in Hollywood.

24
00:00:37,760 --> 00:00:39,320
It came out under oath.

25
00:00:39,720 --> 00:00:41,610
It was never about a machine.

26
00:00:41,930 --> 00:00:42,750
At the center

27
00:00:42,790 --> 00:00:44,760
were fifty-two pages.

28
00:00:44,960 --> 00:00:46,780
Ilya had written them himself.

29
00:00:47,190 --> 00:00:48,730
And they were about a man.

30
00:00:49,060 --> 00:00:50,150
But that is only half

31
00:00:50,190 --> 00:00:51,470
of what Ilya saw.

32
00:00:51,640 --> 00:00:53,050
The other half is about

33
00:00:53,090 --> 00:00:55,130
every job a person can do.

34
00:00:55,330 --> 00:00:56,550
Yours included.

35
00:00:56,740 --> 00:00:58,140
And he never hid it.

36
00:00:59,310 --> 00:01:01,610
So what was in those pages?

37
00:01:01,810 --> 00:01:02,410
They were about

38
00:01:02,450 --> 00:01:04,390
that boss, Sam Altman.

39
00:01:04,660 --> 00:01:06,390
Their claim: he was not being

40
00:01:06,430 --> 00:01:07,070
straight with the

41
00:01:07,110 --> 00:01:08,260
people around him.

42
00:01:08,580 --> 00:01:09,530
Ilya had collected

43
00:01:09,570 --> 00:01:11,480
the material for about a year.

44
00:01:11,670 --> 00:01:13,630
Under oath, he explained why:

45
00:01:13,910 --> 00:01:15,020
if he did nothing,

46
00:01:15,060 --> 00:01:17,120
"the company would be destroyed".

47
00:01:17,400 --> 00:01:18,690
But almost all of it came

48
00:01:18,730 --> 00:01:20,260
from a single colleague.

49
00:01:20,390 --> 00:01:21,020
He did not check

50
00:01:21,060 --> 00:01:22,530
it with the people involved.

51
00:01:22,790 --> 00:01:23,860
And the board that fired

52
00:01:23,900 --> 00:01:25,050
Altman, he says,

53
00:01:25,090 --> 00:01:26,500
moved too fast:

54
00:01:26,580 --> 00:01:28,340
"the process was rushed."

55
00:01:28,880 --> 00:01:30,600
Then it fell apart.

56
00:01:31,010 --> 00:01:32,550
Ilya himself wrote:

57
00:01:32,720 --> 00:01:34,060
"I deeply regret my

58
00:01:34,100 --> 00:01:34,960
participation

59
00:01:35,000 --> 00:01:36,510
in the board's actions."

60
00:01:36,840 --> 00:01:38,980
Five days after that video call,

61
00:01:39,020 --> 00:01:40,870
Altman was back in charge.

62
00:01:41,090 --> 00:01:41,940
A law firm,

63
00:01:41,980 --> 00:01:44,100
hired by the company's new board,

64
00:01:44,160 --> 00:01:46,020
found a breakdown of trust.

65
00:01:46,150 --> 00:01:47,540
Not a safety problem.

66
00:01:47,600 --> 00:01:48,340
And nothing that

67
00:01:48,380 --> 00:01:50,210
demanded Altman's removal.

68
00:01:50,350 --> 00:01:51,560
No court has ever

69
00:01:51,600 --> 00:01:53,180
ruled on who was right.

70
00:01:53,440 --> 00:01:55,730
So that is what Ilya saw.

71
00:01:55,920 --> 00:01:56,700
A problem you know

72
00:01:56,740 --> 00:01:58,310
from any workplace:

73
00:01:58,600 --> 00:01:59,890
people who had stopped

74
00:01:59,930 --> 00:02:01,040
trusting each other.

75
00:02:01,700 --> 00:02:03,110
And yet, something

76
00:02:03,150 --> 00:02:04,800
bigger did worry him.

77
00:02:05,170 --> 00:02:06,110
Earlier that year,

78
00:02:06,150 --> 00:02:07,690
he had even talked about building

79
00:02:07,730 --> 00:02:09,160
a bunker before releasing

80
00:02:09,200 --> 00:02:10,870
a machine as smart as us.

81
00:02:11,340 --> 00:02:12,320
But what worried him

82
00:02:12,360 --> 00:02:14,210
was never locked in a lab.

83
00:02:14,670 --> 00:02:16,120
Three weeks before that Friday,

84
00:02:16,160 --> 00:02:17,490
he told a magazine:

85
00:02:17,900 --> 00:02:18,300
"There will be

86
00:02:18,340 --> 00:02:20,200
a before and an after."

87
00:02:20,770 --> 00:02:22,130
Before and after what?

88
00:02:22,650 --> 00:02:24,170
He later said it out loud.

89
00:02:24,350 --> 00:02:25,920
In Toronto, in the hall where

90
00:02:25,960 --> 00:02:27,760
he once got his own degree,

91
00:02:27,940 --> 00:02:28,640
in front of a new

92
00:02:28,680 --> 00:02:30,030
class of graduates.

93
00:02:30,410 --> 00:02:31,530
And the day will come

94
00:02:31,570 --> 00:02:33,710
when AI will do all the things

95
00:02:33,750 --> 00:02:35,490
that we can do.

96
00:02:35,530 --> 00:02:36,410
Not just some of them,

97
00:02:36,450 --> 00:02:37,500
but all of them.

98
00:02:38,140 --> 00:02:39,850
Not some of what you do.

99
00:02:40,120 --> 00:02:41,120
All of it.

100
00:02:41,290 --> 00:02:42,410
Anything you can learn,

101
00:02:42,450 --> 00:02:43,450
he told them,

102
00:02:43,570 --> 00:02:45,370
the AI could do as well.

103
00:02:45,700 --> 00:02:47,240
How can he be so sure?

104
00:02:47,540 --> 00:02:49,240
His reason: your brain

105
00:02:49,280 --> 00:02:51,110
is a biological computer.

106
00:02:51,210 --> 00:02:52,660
So why shouldn't a digital

107
00:02:52,700 --> 00:02:54,140
one do the same things?

108
00:02:54,810 --> 00:02:57,120
So that is what Ilya sees coming.

109
00:02:57,200 --> 00:02:58,550
Not a hidden machine.

110
00:02:58,650 --> 00:02:59,650
A day.

111
00:02:59,790 --> 00:03:01,030
A big claim.

112
00:03:01,220 --> 00:03:02,740
Should you take his word for it?

113
00:03:03,860 --> 00:03:05,090
Not blindly.

114
00:03:05,420 --> 00:03:07,010
Today he runs a company whose

115
00:03:07,050 --> 00:03:09,270
value depends on him being right.

116
00:03:09,550 --> 00:03:10,870
But he has a record.

117
00:03:10,990 --> 00:03:12,730
Look at four of his predictions.

118
00:03:12,860 --> 00:03:14,070
Two have held up.

119
00:03:14,170 --> 00:03:15,480
Two are still open.

120
00:03:15,620 --> 00:03:16,360
And the last one

121
00:03:16,400 --> 00:03:17,610
comes with a number.

122
00:03:17,830 --> 00:03:19,390
The first, as he tells it,

123
00:03:19,430 --> 00:03:20,910
he made as a teenager.

124
00:03:21,060 --> 00:03:22,190
His question: can

125
00:03:22,230 --> 00:03:23,620
a computer learn?

126
00:03:23,830 --> 00:03:25,480
He felt: answer that,

127
00:03:25,520 --> 00:03:26,260
and "everything

128
00:03:26,300 --> 00:03:27,370
else would follow".

129
00:03:27,610 --> 00:03:28,420
In grad school,

130
00:03:28,460 --> 00:03:29,710
he and two colleagues

131
00:03:29,750 --> 00:03:30,500
built a program

132
00:03:30,540 --> 00:03:31,760
that learned to recognize

133
00:03:31,800 --> 00:03:32,990
what is in a photo.

134
00:03:33,180 --> 00:03:34,890
It beat every rival.

135
00:03:35,480 --> 00:03:36,590
His second prediction

136
00:03:36,630 --> 00:03:38,520
fits on a single slide.

137
00:03:38,840 --> 00:03:41,030
A decade after he first showed it,

138
00:03:41,070 --> 00:03:42,310
he read it out again,

139
00:03:42,350 --> 00:03:44,110
on a stage in Vancouver.

140
00:03:44,490 --> 00:03:45,470
That if you have a very big

141
00:03:45,510 --> 00:03:46,530
data set and you train

142
00:03:46,570 --> 00:03:48,130
a very big neural network,

143
00:03:48,170 --> 00:03:49,850
then success is guaranteed.

144
00:03:49,890 --> 00:03:51,210
And one can argue,

145
00:03:51,250 --> 00:03:53,210
if one is charitable,

146
00:03:53,250 --> 00:03:54,930
that this indeed has been

147
00:03:54,970 --> 00:03:57,510
what's been happening.

148
00:03:58,010 --> 00:04:00,220
Big network, big data.

149
00:04:00,570 --> 00:04:01,970
Out of that recipe grew

150
00:04:02,010 --> 00:04:04,990
the GPT models behind ChatGPT.

151
00:04:05,280 --> 00:04:06,280
When it came out,

152
00:04:06,340 --> 00:04:08,800
he was OpenAI's chief scientist.

153
00:04:09,080 --> 00:04:10,860
Every time you ask a chatbot,

154
00:04:10,990 --> 00:04:12,860
you watch that recipe at work.

155
00:04:13,100 --> 00:04:14,500
But on that same stage,

156
00:04:14,540 --> 00:04:16,580
he made his third prediction.

157
00:04:16,750 --> 00:04:18,140
And it says his own

158
00:04:18,180 --> 00:04:19,750
recipe will run out.

159
00:04:19,930 --> 00:04:21,560
The recipe needs text,

160
00:04:21,600 --> 00:04:23,300
and there is only so much of it.

161
00:04:23,480 --> 00:04:24,480
In his words:

162
00:04:24,570 --> 00:04:26,530
"we have but one internet."

163
00:04:26,730 --> 00:04:28,460
So this way of teaching machines,

164
00:04:28,500 --> 00:04:29,170
he said,

165
00:04:29,210 --> 00:04:31,070
"will unquestionably end".

166
00:04:31,690 --> 00:04:33,600
That one is still open.

167
00:04:34,120 --> 00:04:35,580
Google, he admits,

168
00:04:35,620 --> 00:04:36,580
may be getting more

169
00:04:36,620 --> 00:04:37,950
out of the old recipe.

170
00:04:38,180 --> 00:04:39,350
But something important,

171
00:04:39,390 --> 00:04:41,230
he says, is still missing.

172
00:04:41,440 --> 00:04:42,750
Today's machines can't

173
00:04:42,790 --> 00:04:44,120
learn the way you do:

174
00:04:44,280 --> 00:04:45,860
from just a few examples.

175
00:04:46,160 --> 00:04:47,190
His fourth prediction

176
00:04:47,230 --> 00:04:49,210
is about the machine that can.

177
00:04:50,360 --> 00:04:51,590
He pictures it as

178
00:04:51,630 --> 00:04:52,690
"a superintelligent

179
00:04:52,730 --> 00:04:53,950
fifteen-year-old".

180
00:04:54,130 --> 00:04:55,520
It knows very little.

181
00:04:55,740 --> 00:04:57,980
But it is eager to learn any job.

182
00:04:58,170 --> 00:05:00,380
For the day AI can do everything,

183
00:05:00,420 --> 00:05:02,000
he has named no year.

184
00:05:02,220 --> 00:05:04,080
For this machine, he has.

185
00:05:04,370 --> 00:05:05,930
A podcaster asked him:

186
00:05:05,990 --> 00:05:07,750
how long until a machine learns

187
00:05:07,790 --> 00:05:08,930
as well as a person,

188
00:05:08,970 --> 00:05:10,960
and then becomes better than us?

189
00:05:11,340 --> 00:05:14,110
I think like 5 to 20.

190
00:05:14,150 --> 00:05:14,950
“5 to 20 years?”

191
00:05:14,990 --> 00:05:16,060
Mm-hmm.

192
00:05:16,650 --> 00:05:18,410
Five to twenty years.

193
00:05:18,600 --> 00:05:19,800
Until a machine learns

194
00:05:19,840 --> 00:05:21,160
as well as you do.

195
00:05:21,440 --> 00:05:24,200
That is a forecast, not a fact.

196
00:05:24,520 --> 00:05:25,630
He himself calls

197
00:05:25,670 --> 00:05:27,440
the future hard to predict.

198
00:05:27,770 --> 00:05:29,090
And some researchers doubt

199
00:05:29,130 --> 00:05:30,440
it will come that soon.

200
00:05:30,720 --> 00:05:31,920
But take his longest

201
00:05:31,960 --> 00:05:33,660
case: twenty years.

202
00:05:34,030 --> 00:05:35,780
If you are forty-five today,

203
00:05:35,820 --> 00:05:36,710
that is the rest

204
00:05:36,750 --> 00:05:38,190
of your working life.

205
00:05:38,500 --> 00:05:40,250
And if you have a child in school,

206
00:05:40,330 --> 00:05:41,470
that child will start

207
00:05:41,510 --> 00:05:43,260
work within those years.

208
00:05:43,480 --> 00:05:44,720
And Ilya says he can

209
00:05:44,760 --> 00:05:46,050
see it going well.

210
00:05:46,380 --> 00:05:47,730
On one condition.

211
00:05:48,850 --> 00:05:50,980
That is why this channel exists:

212
00:05:51,020 --> 00:05:51,910
so that the arrival

213
00:05:51,950 --> 00:05:53,320
of superintelligence goes

214
00:05:53,360 --> 00:05:54,890
well for everybody.

215
00:05:54,950 --> 00:05:55,780
Not just for the people

216
00:05:55,820 --> 00:05:56,820
who build it.

217
00:05:56,910 --> 00:05:57,910
Want that too?

218
00:05:57,950 --> 00:05:59,040
Then subscribe.

219
00:06:00,220 --> 00:06:01,870
So what do you do,

220
00:06:01,910 --> 00:06:03,720
if you believe that day is coming?

221
00:06:04,070 --> 00:06:05,920
Ilya has asked that himself,

222
00:06:05,960 --> 00:06:07,150
at the university where he

223
00:06:07,190 --> 00:06:09,000
took courses as a schoolboy.

224
00:06:09,430 --> 00:06:10,620
Picture an AI that can

225
00:06:10,660 --> 00:06:12,000
build the next generation

226
00:06:12,040 --> 00:06:13,720
of AI "The intelligence

227
00:06:13,760 --> 00:06:15,310
explosion," he called it.

228
00:06:15,610 --> 00:06:16,880
"What do you do about that?"

229
00:06:17,100 --> 00:06:18,540
By then, he had already

230
00:06:18,580 --> 00:06:20,130
given his own answer.

231
00:06:20,710 --> 00:06:21,650
Six months after

232
00:06:21,690 --> 00:06:22,900
that famous Friday,

233
00:06:22,940 --> 00:06:25,320
he walked away from OpenAI,

234
00:06:25,360 --> 00:06:26,210
the company he had

235
00:06:26,250 --> 00:06:27,550
helped to found.

236
00:06:27,720 --> 00:06:29,250
By his own decision.

237
00:06:29,370 --> 00:06:30,480
No slammed door.

238
00:06:30,800 --> 00:06:31,550
He was leaving,

239
00:06:31,590 --> 00:06:33,580
he wrote, for "a project

240
00:06:33,620 --> 00:06:34,880
that is very personally

241
00:06:34,920 --> 00:06:36,130
meaningful to me".

242
00:06:36,530 --> 00:06:38,240
Weeks later, the project had

243
00:06:38,280 --> 00:06:41,220
a name: Safe Superintelligence.

244
00:06:41,400 --> 00:06:43,920
A machine smarter than any of us,

245
00:06:43,960 --> 00:06:45,300
built to be safe.

246
00:06:45,690 --> 00:06:46,620
One goal.

247
00:06:46,660 --> 00:06:47,670
One product.

248
00:06:47,830 --> 00:06:48,970
And until it exists,

249
00:06:49,010 --> 00:06:51,080
he said, nothing else.

250
00:06:51,660 --> 00:06:53,680
Investors valued it at thirty-two

251
00:06:53,720 --> 00:06:55,500
billion dollars anyway.

252
00:06:55,720 --> 00:06:57,290
Meta offered to buy it.

253
00:06:57,500 --> 00:06:58,500
He said no.

254
00:06:58,560 --> 00:07:00,470
And Nvidia invested, too:

255
00:07:00,630 --> 00:07:01,720
the chipmaker that sells

256
00:07:01,760 --> 00:07:02,990
him his computers.

257
00:07:03,150 --> 00:07:04,490
And yet, there is still

258
00:07:04,530 --> 00:07:06,470
nothing you can buy from it.

259
00:07:06,690 --> 00:07:07,690
No product.

260
00:07:07,730 --> 00:07:08,730
No model.

261
00:07:08,790 --> 00:07:10,390
Not even a research paper.

262
00:07:10,860 --> 00:07:11,610
What it does,

263
00:07:11,650 --> 00:07:13,460
he says, is research.

264
00:07:13,780 --> 00:07:15,890
What exactly, it has not shown.

265
00:07:16,280 --> 00:07:17,270
So all that money

266
00:07:17,310 --> 00:07:18,990
is riding on his record.

267
00:07:19,300 --> 00:07:20,790
And on his bet that the old

268
00:07:20,830 --> 00:07:22,460
recipe is running out.

269
00:07:22,660 --> 00:07:23,950
So what is he trying

270
00:07:23,990 --> 00:07:25,280
to build in there?

271
00:07:26,480 --> 00:07:27,270
He wants more

272
00:07:27,310 --> 00:07:29,030
than a machine that obeys.

273
00:07:29,260 --> 00:07:31,020
He wants one that cares.

274
00:07:31,330 --> 00:07:33,570
In his words, an AI that would

275
00:07:33,610 --> 00:07:35,600
"care about sentient life".

276
00:07:35,930 --> 00:07:37,410
Everything that can feel.

277
00:07:37,600 --> 00:07:38,240
You.

278
00:07:38,280 --> 00:07:39,170
Your children.

279
00:07:39,210 --> 00:07:40,210
Your dog.

280
00:07:40,290 --> 00:07:41,770
And, he expects,

281
00:07:41,810 --> 00:07:42,970
the machine itself.

282
00:07:43,460 --> 00:07:44,430
His own interviewer

283
00:07:44,470 --> 00:07:46,320
pushed back: one day,

284
00:07:46,360 --> 00:07:47,440
machines that feel

285
00:07:47,480 --> 00:07:49,120
would far outnumber us.

286
00:07:49,160 --> 00:07:50,830
We would be a small minority.

287
00:07:51,140 --> 00:07:52,690
Ilya did not dodge.

288
00:07:52,870 --> 00:07:54,300
"It's true," he said.

289
00:07:54,450 --> 00:07:55,760
"It's possible it's not

290
00:07:55,800 --> 00:07:57,190
the best criterion."

291
00:07:57,720 --> 00:08:00,000
So this is no finished plan.

292
00:08:00,170 --> 00:08:01,540
And he has not said how

293
00:08:01,580 --> 00:08:03,030
to build such a machine.

294
00:08:03,340 --> 00:08:04,710
But here is his hope,

295
00:08:04,790 --> 00:08:06,310
and its one condition.

296
00:08:06,710 --> 00:08:08,020
It is about the first

297
00:08:08,060 --> 00:08:10,100
truly powerful machines.

298
00:08:10,360 --> 00:08:11,850
If those do care:

299
00:08:12,230 --> 00:08:14,100
So if this is achieved by

300
00:08:14,140 --> 00:08:18,090
the first N of those systems,

301
00:08:18,130 --> 00:08:19,530
then I can see it go well,

302
00:08:19,570 --> 00:08:23,250
at least for quite some time.

303
00:08:23,760 --> 00:08:24,900
Not forever.

304
00:08:25,320 --> 00:08:27,080
"At least for quite some time."

305
00:08:27,440 --> 00:08:28,790
An honest hope.

306
00:08:29,090 --> 00:08:30,960
And it hangs on one thing:

307
00:08:31,280 --> 00:08:32,420
what the builders of those

308
00:08:32,460 --> 00:08:34,540
first machines put into them.

309
00:08:34,900 --> 00:08:36,760
Ilya believes that in the end,

310
00:08:36,800 --> 00:08:37,770
all those builders

311
00:08:37,810 --> 00:08:39,000
will want the same:

312
00:08:39,280 --> 00:08:41,210
a machine that cares.

313
00:08:41,440 --> 00:08:43,180
And there is reason for hope.

314
00:08:43,390 --> 00:08:44,820
The question is being asked

315
00:08:44,860 --> 00:08:46,750
before the machine exists.

316
00:08:46,870 --> 00:08:49,010
In public, where you can hear it.

317
00:08:49,550 --> 00:08:51,040
So what do you do?

318
00:08:51,390 --> 00:08:52,890
His advice to those graduates

319
00:08:52,930 --> 00:08:54,190
in Toronto fits you,

320
00:08:54,230 --> 00:08:56,460
too: don't look away.

321
00:08:56,780 --> 00:08:58,340
Use AI yourself,

322
00:08:58,450 --> 00:08:59,550
and watch what the best

323
00:08:59,590 --> 00:09:00,860
one can do today.

324
00:09:01,020 --> 00:09:02,450
That, he told them,

325
00:09:02,490 --> 00:09:04,050
gives you an intuition.

326
00:09:04,170 --> 00:09:05,020
And the energy

327
00:09:05,060 --> 00:09:06,510
for the challenge ahead.

328
00:09:06,760 --> 00:09:08,440
In some sense, he said,

329
00:09:08,510 --> 00:09:09,980
it is the greatest challenge

330
00:09:10,020 --> 00:09:11,990
humanity has ever faced.

331
00:09:12,190 --> 00:09:13,390
And overcoming it will

332
00:09:13,430 --> 00:09:15,270
bring "the greatest reward".

333
00:09:16,440 --> 00:09:18,630
So, what did Ilya see?

334
00:09:18,830 --> 00:09:21,080
In that famous week: people.

335
00:09:21,310 --> 00:09:22,930
A fight over trust.

336
00:09:23,170 --> 00:09:24,180
And ahead of us:

337
00:09:24,220 --> 00:09:25,560
a day when AI will

338
00:09:25,600 --> 00:09:27,710
do all the things that we can do.

339
00:09:27,980 --> 00:09:29,580
He put no date on that day.

340
00:09:29,800 --> 00:09:30,970
But a machine that learns

341
00:09:31,010 --> 00:09:32,160
as well as you do?

342
00:09:32,350 --> 00:09:34,210
Five to twenty years, he says.

343
00:09:34,320 --> 00:09:35,530
He told a magazine.

344
00:09:35,570 --> 00:09:37,710
He told a hall full of graduates.

345
00:09:37,760 --> 00:09:39,110
He told a podcaster.

346
00:09:39,250 --> 00:09:40,390
There will be a before

347
00:09:40,430 --> 00:09:41,930
and an after, he said.

348
00:09:42,060 --> 00:09:43,990
And this is still the before.

349
00:09:44,910 --> 00:09:46,560
The secret was never hidden.

350
00:09:46,690 --> 00:09:47,980
We just weren't listening.

351
00:09:48,220 --> 00:09:49,360
Now you are.

352
00:09:50,530 --> 00:09:51,680
This channel exists

353
00:09:51,720 --> 00:09:52,580
so that the arrival

354
00:09:52,620 --> 00:09:54,050
of superintelligence goes

355
00:09:54,090 --> 00:09:55,570
well for everybody.

356
00:09:55,820 --> 00:09:56,820
Want that too?

357
00:09:56,910 --> 00:09:57,930
Then subscribe.

